# 5. How Java code runs

Suppose you have:

```text
Hello.java
```

Compile:

```bash
javac Hello.java
```

You get:

```text
Hello.class
```

Then:

```bash
java Hello
```

The JVM loads and executes the bytecode.

Conceptually:

```text
             Java source
                 │
                 │ javac
                 ↓
          Java bytecode
            Hello.class
                 │
                 │
                 ↓
                JVM
                 │
        ┌────────┼────────┐
        ↓        ↓        ↓
      Linux   Windows   macOS
```

This is fundamentally different from traditional native compilation.

For example:

```text
C
C source
  ↓
compiler
  ↓
native machine code
  ↓
CPU
```

while Java traditionally follows:

```text
Java source
    ↓
bytecode
    ↓
JVM
    ↓
native execution
```

Modern JVMs use **JIT (Just-In-Time) compilation** to compile frequently executed bytecode into native machine code at runtime.

