# 6. Java's major programming characteristics

Java is particularly known for several characteristics.

## Object-oriented

Java was designed around object-oriented programming.

For example:

```java
class Dog {
    String name;

    void bark() {
        System.out.println(name + " says woof");
    }
}
```

Then:

```java
Dog dog = new Dog();
dog.name = "Buddy";
dog.bark();
```

Java strongly emphasizes:

```text
Class
 ↓
Object
 ↓
State + Behavior
```

---

## Statically typed

Java variables have declared types:

```java
int age = 20;
String name = "Mike";
boolean active = true;
```

The compiler checks types.

For example:

```java
int age = "hello";  // compilation error
```

This is different from dynamically typed languages such as JavaScript and Python.

---

## Garbage collected

Java normally manages memory automatically.

```java
Dog dog = new Dog();
```

You don't normally need:

```c
free(dog);
```

Instead, the JVM's **Garbage Collector** identifies objects that are no longer reachable and eventually reclaims their memory.

Conceptually:

```text
Application
    ↓
creates objects
    ↓
Heap
    ↓
objects become unreachable
    ↓
Garbage Collector
    ↓
memory reclaimed
```

---

## Multithreaded

Java has had built-in concurrency support since its early days.

For example:

```java
Thread thread = new Thread(() -> {
    System.out.println("Hello from another thread");
});

thread.start();
```

Modern Java also has **virtual threads**, introduced as a major feature through Project Loom.

For backend development, concurrency is especially important.

---

## Portable

Java bytecode is designed to be executed by JVM implementations on different operating systems.

```text
             Java program
                  ↓
              bytecode
                  ↓
              JVM API
        ┌─────────┼─────────┐
        ↓         ↓         ↓
      Linux    Windows    macOS
```

This portability was one of Java's original major advantages.

